  "text": "We are given two curves:\n\n1. $ y = ax^3 - x^2 + 5 $\n2. $ y = x^2 + 2x - 3 $\n\nWe are asked to find the value of $ a $ such that **the graphs have exactly one common tangent line**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find the common tangent line\n\nTwo curves have a **common tangent line** if there exists a line $ y = mx + c $ that satisfies:\n\n- It is tangent to both curves.\n\nThis means the line must satisfy the equations of both curves, and the system should have exactly one solution (i.e., the line is tangent to both curves).\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Find the condition for a common tangent\n\nWe can find the tangent line to both curves.\n\nLet’s suppose the common tangent is $ y = mx + c $.\n\n#### 1. Tangency condition to the first curve:\n\nWe can find the point of tangency on $ y = ax^3 - x^2 + 5 $.\n\nDifferentiate the first curve:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx} = 3ax^2 - 2x\n$$\n\nAt the point of tangency, the slope must equal the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\nm = 3ax^2 - 2x\n$$\n\nAlso, the value of the function at the point of tangency must be equal to the value of the tangent line:\n\n$$\nax^3 - x^2 + 5 = mx + c\n$$\n\nSo we have:\n\n- $ m = 3ax^2 - 2x $\n- $ c = ax^3 - x^2 + 5 - m = ax^3 - x^2 + 5 - (3ax^2 - 2x) = ax^3 - x^2 + 5 - 3ax^2 + 2x $\n\nThus, the tangent line is:\n\n$$\ny = mx + c = (3ax^2 - 2x) x + (ax^3 - x^2 + 5 - 3ax^2 + 2x)\n$$\n\nSimplify:\n\n$$\ny = 3a x^3 - 2x^2 + ax^3 - x^2 + 5 + 2x = (3a + a)x^3 - (2 + 1)x^2 + 2x + 5\n$$\n$$\ny = 4a x^3 - 3x^2 + 2x + 5\n$$\n\nSo the tangent line is:\n\n$$\ny = mx + c = (3ax^2 - 2x)x + (ax^3 - x^2 + 5 - 3ax^2 + 2x)\n$$\n\nSimplify again:\n\n$$\ny = (3a x^3 - 2x^2) + (ax^3 - x^2 + 5 - 3a x^2 + 2x)\n$$\n$$\ny = (3a + a)x^3 + (-2 - 1 - 3a)x^2 + 2x + 5\n$$\n$$\ny = 4a x^3 - 3x^2 + 2x + 5\n$$\n\nSo the **tangent line** is:\n\n$$\ny = 4a x^3 - 3x^2 + 2x + 5\n$$\n\nNow, the second curve is:\n\n$$\ny = x^2 + 2x - 3\n$$\n\nWe want this tangent line to be tangent to the second curve. So we set:\n\n$$\n4a x^3 - 3x^2 + 2x + 5 = x^2 + 2x - 3\n$$\n\nSubtract the right-hand side from both sides:\n\n$$\n4a x^3 - 3x^2 + 2x + 5 - x^2 - 2x + 3 = 0\n$$\n$$\n4a x^3 - 4x^2 + 8 = 0\n$$\n\nFactor:\n\n$$\n4x^2 (a x - 1) + 8 = 0\n$$\n\nLet’s solve for $ a $:\n\n$$\n4a x^3 - 4x^2 + 8 = 0 \\Rightarrow a x^3 - x^2 + 2 = 0\n$$\n\nSo this is a cubic equation in $ x $, and for the line to be **tangent to the second curve**, this cubic equation must have exactly one real root, and all its roots must be **equal to** the x-coordinate of the point of tangency.\n\nSo we want:\n\n$$\na x^3 - x^2 + 2 = 0 \\text{ has exactly one real root }\n$$\n\nLet’s denote:\n\n$$\nf(x) = a x^3 - x^2 + 2\n$$\n\nWe want $ f(x) = 0 $ to have **exactly one real root**\n\nThis is equivalent to saying that the function $ f(x) $ has **exactly one real root**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Determine the value of $ a $ such that $ f(x) = a x^3 - x^2 + 2 $ has exactly one real root\n\nThis happens when the cubic equation has **exactly one real root**, which is a **double root**.\n\nA cubic has exactly one real root when the discriminant is zero.\n\nRecall that for a cubic $ f(x) = ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d $, the discriminant is:\n\n$$\n\\Delta = 18abcd - 4b^3d + b^2c^2 - 4ac^3 - 27a^2d^2\n$$\n\nLet’s apply this to our function:\n\n- $ a = a $\n- $ b = -1 $\n- $ c = 0 $\n- $ d = 2 $\n\nPlug in:\n\n$$\n\\Delta = 18 \\cdot a \\cdot (-1) \\cdot 0 \\cdot 2 - 4(-1)^3 \\cdot 2 + (-1)^2 \\cdot 0^2 - 4a \\cdot 0^3 - 27a^2 \\cdot 2^2\n$$\n\n$$\n= 0 + 8 + 0 - 0 - 27a^2 \\cdot 4 = 8 - 108a^2\n$$\n\nSo:\n\n$$\n\\Delta = 8 - 108a^2 = 0 \\Rightarrow 8 = 108a^2 \\Rightarrow a^2 = \\frac{8}{108} = \\frac{2}{27}\n$$\n\n$$\na = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{27}} = \\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{9}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{9}}\n$$",
